text(s, 0.9, 5.68, 11.9, 1.1, 'Relief, Recovery, and the Alphabet Agencies at Work', size=30, color=CREAM, font='Georgia')
text(s, 0.9, 6.62, 11.5, 0.5, 'A grocery order, a camp check for twenty five dollars, a winter job, a dam. What the New Deal did at the kitchen table.', size=17, color=POWDER)
notes(s, "Open by moving the unit down from the Capitol to the kitchen table. Lesson 3 ended with laws signed at speed in the Hundred Days. This lesson follows those signatures home: into a relief office deciding a family\'s grocery order, into a camp where a young man\'s pay envelope is split before he sees it, onto a winter work crew, and onto a bridge site that will not finish for years. The photographs in this deck come from the agencies' own records: camp tables, a tree going into the ground, a dam face, and an NRA parade. One distinction organizes everything that follows, and students should write it at the top of their notes now: direct relief against work relief, help given against help earned. Every agency in the lesson stands on one side of that line or works both sides of it, and the closing argument of the lesson, about how far help reached, is graded agency by agency along the line.")
# 2 QUESTION
s = slide(NAVY)
kicker(s, 'The question for today')
text(s, 0.9, 1.9, 11.5, 3.4, [[('What did the New Deal agencies actually do in people\'s lives, and how far did their help reach?', {})]], size=42, color=CREAM, font='Georgia', leading=1.25)
rule(s, 0.92, 5.6)
text(s, 0.92, 5.85, 11.5, 0.9, [[('Unit question: ', {'color': RUST_LT, 'italic': True}), ('How did the New Deal change the relationship between Americans and their federal government, and what did it leave undone?', {'italic': True})]], size=17, color=POWDER, font='Georgia', leading=1.2)
notes(s, "Read the lesson question aloud and point at its two halves, because the exit ticket and the writing task both grade the two halves separately. The first half is concrete: what did the agencies actually do, in named lives, with dollar figures attached. A grocery order is an answer. A monthly check of twenty five dollars sent home is an answer. A speech about recovery is not. The second half is a measurement: how far did the help reach, against the size of the need. That measurement must be honest in both directions. Millions helped is a fact. One worker in five still jobless in 1934 is also a fact, and an answer that carries only one of them has not answered the question this lesson asks. Connect the question to the unit question beneath it. This lesson supplies the unit\'s first evidence about what the New Deal left undone: the gaps in delivery, the groups outside the designs, and the unemployment that no agency of 1933 cured.")
