notes(s, "This slide mirrors the closing table of the guided notes, and students should complete that table now, agency by agency, from memory where they can. The rows are grouped by the lesson spine: direct relief first, in the FERA row; work relief next, split between the fast programs, CCC and CWA, and the slow building program, PWA; and the recovery experiment, the NRA, last, because it treated the economy rather than the household. Check the table with the two questions that expose shallow answers. First, which row would a widowed mother with three small children appear in, and why can it be no other row? The FERA row, because camp, construction, and code all presuppose a worker who can leave home. Second, which rows ended or were designed to end quickly, and what did ending cost? The CWA row above all, whose spring shutdown returned millions to direct relief. Grade the table generously on wording and strictly on the relief distinction, because the quick check and the case files grade the same distinction in application.")
