notes(s, "Close by handing the lesson to the work. In the activity, Agency Case Files, each group receives one American: a jobless young man in Ohio, a widowed mother in Chicago, a drought farmer in Oklahoma, a laid off teacher in Mississippi, and a construction engineer in Oregon, and matches the case to the agency built for it, states exactly what that agency would provide, and records who in the file no agency reaches and why. The notebook page rebuilds the agencies as a chart of relief kinds and missed groups. The quick check, Relief at Work, tests the relief distinction, the CCC wage, and the unemployment table against blueprint entries seven and eight. The writing task, How Far Did Help Reach, requires the ledger judgment of this lesson in essay form. The exit ticket has three parts: the difference between direct relief and work relief in one sentence, two work relief agencies with what each built or did, and the lesson question answered in two or three sentences Next lesson takes up the critics of 1935, who called this same record far too little and far too much.")

